Carafe: one state word per thread says where it is for SuspendThread: asked to stop or end, inside the kernel,
held there, or just leaving it. A thread inside a system call is in the kernel; when it is suspended the server
holds it there at once, whether it waits or runs, and the thread stops when it leaves the call and takes any
registers set meanwhile. A thread suspended on its way into a call stops at the entry. Every change is made in one
step on the word and signalled on it, so a resume can never be missed by a thread about to hold itself and the
server waits for the word instead of a timer.
LGPL-2.1-or-later, as the file it changes.

